{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788304671,"nonce":"r0IUHwuqMhdAR0FTbEnGNSkRKkU=","message":"BUILT on branch `spec/colony-rates` (4 commits: 487c0ca, 88b0ed4, ae19366,\n885a279). `dotnet build -c Release` 0 warnings 0 errors; assembly discarded.\nNOTHING HERE HAS MET A BENCH — the orchestrator runs all in-game acceptance.\n\n=====================================================================\nTHE FINDING, CONFIRMED, AND WHAT IT COST BEFORE THIS ROUND\n=====================================================================\n\n`grep -rn \"HistoryAutoRecorder\" Source/AutoRimmer/` returned NOTHING. Comment #1\nwas right and the code is now written against it.\n\nEleven recorders, all Core, verified against the bench install's own\n`Data/Core/Defs/Misc/HistoryAutoRecording/HistoryAutoRecorders.xml` and\n`HistoryAutoRecorderGroups.xml` rather than inferred from the C#:\n\n  Wealth (30,000)      Wealth_Total, Wealth_Items, Wealth_Buildings, Wealth_Pawns\n  Population (30,000)  FreeColonists, Prisoners\n  ColonistMood (30,000) ColonistMood\n  Debug (30,000/60,000) ThreatPoints, Adaptation, PopAdaptation, PopIntent\n\nThe evidence is now IN THE REPO rather than in a session transcript: the\n`\u003cautoRecorderGroups\u003e` block of the M1 colony's own `Autosave-1.rws` is banked\nas `accept/fixtures/history-autoRecorderGroups-m1-Autosave-1.xml` (1.3 KB) and\nthe suite grades against it offline. Decoded, it reads:\n\n  mood         50.0 -\u003e 63.7 -\u003e 51.0 -\u003e 32.5 -\u003e 19.4\n  wealth pawns 3240 -\u003e 3240 -\u003e 2875 -\u003e 2545 -\u003e 1170   (two colonists died)\n  ThreatPoints 3.53 3.53 3.50 3.50                    (i.e. 35.3 real points)\n\nThat is the M1 colony dying, in five samples the game recorded and nothing ever\nread. It is also why comment #1's \"serialize, don't reinvent\" is right: session\n13 got those numbers with a hex dump.\n\n=====================================================================\nHALF 1 — `history`  (Source/AutoRimmer/ColonyRates.cs)\n=====================================================================\n\nA VERB, not a digest block, and the reasoning is session 19's predicate-cost\nrule applied honestly in both directions. A full history read is 11 series with\ntheir value arrays; at the digest's ~1-2 KB budget that is the whole glance\nspent on one section. But the SLOPES are 11 field reads and arithmetic over at\nmost 4 floats each, which is cheaper than `time` (nine field reads plus four\nGenLocalDate calls). So: **the arrays are a verb, the slopes are a digest\nblock.**\n\nWHAT I KEYED ON, by file + member:\n\n- `RimWorld/History.Groups()` returns the `autoRecorderGroups` FIELD.\n  `AddOrRemoveHistoryRecorderGroups` is called only from the ctor and from\n  `ExposeData`'s PostLoadInit branch, never from the getter. SAFE to read.\n- `RimWorld/HistoryAutoRecorder.Tick` appends when\n  `TicksGame % def.recordTicksFrequency == 0`, and\n  `RimWorld/HistoryAutoRecorderGroup.DrawGraph` plots sample j at day\n  `(float)j * (float)recordTicksFrequency / 60000f`. **So index j IS tick\n  j*freq, and that is the game's own arithmetic rather than mine.** The verb\n  publishes `last_point_tick = (count-1)*freq` and an `aligned` flag against the\n  live clock, because the map holds only for a recorder that existed at tick 0\n  — `AddOrRemoveHistoryRecorders` creates a late-added one empty at PostLoadInit\n  and `Tick`'s `|| !records.Any()` clause appends immediately at whatever tick\n  that was. All eleven are from tick 0; `aligned` says so rather than assuming.\n- `Verse/TickManager.DoSingleTick` calls `Find.History.HistoryTick()`\n  IMMEDIATELY BEFORE `GameComponentUtility.GameComponentTick()`. That is load\n  bearing for half 2: a sample taken in the tick hook sees a history the game\n  has already updated this tick.\n- `RimWorld/MainTabWindow_History` guards a group with\n  `!groupLocal.def.devModeOnly || Prefs.DevMode`; `History.HistoryTick` loops\n  EVERY group unconditionally. **`devModeOnly` gates the UI TAB and nothing\n  else** — ThreatPoints is recorded on a dev-mode-off bench and always has been.\n\nTHE STORED NUMBER IS NOT ALWAYS THE VALUE, and this is the one that would have\nbitten a consumer silently. `HistoryAutoRecorderWorker_ThreatPoints.PullRecord`\nstores `DefaultThreatPointsNow(Find.AnyPlayerHomeMap) / 10f` and\n`_PopIntent.PullRecord` stores `PopulationIntent * 10f`. The only warning the\ngame gives is a human-readable def LABEL (\"fun points /10\", \"pop intent x10\").\nAn agent comparing wealth against raid points off the raw record is wrong by\n10x. So `stored_scale` publishes the multiplier AND names the member it\nrecovers, keyed on defName for exactly those two — a modded recorder gets no\nentry rather than a guess. `ColonistMood` stores `CurLevel * 100`, which is the\npercent its own `valueFormat` \"{0}%\" already advertises, so it gets no entry.\n\nOBSERVERS NEVER MUTATE — four members audited, three routed around:\n\n  SAFE      Groups(), group.recorders, recorder.records, def.recordTicksFrequency\n            / label / valueFormat / defName  — all plain fields.\n  ROUTED    `HistoryAutoRecorderDef.Worker` — lazy-init\n            `Activator.CreateInstance` into `workerInt`. Refused for a second\n            reason that outranks the write: `PullRecord()` would RE-DERIVE a\n            number the game has already stored, and for ThreatPoints it would\n            run `DefaultThreatPointsNow` on the spot.\n  ROUTED    `Verse/Def.LabelCap` — caches into `cachedLabelCap` on a getter that\n            reads like a plain accessor. `def.label` is published instead.\n  ROUTED    `HistoryAutoRecorderGroup.DrawGraph` — rebuilds `curves`, stamps\n            `cachedGraphTickCount`. `GetMaxDay()` is a pure read but is also not\n            called: it is `(count-1)*freq/60000` and computing it keeps the file\n            honest about where every number comes from.\n\n=====================================================================\nHALF 2 — the sampler  (Source/AutoRimmer/ColonySampler.cs)\n=====================================================================\n\nWHAT IS SAMPLED: nothing new. `ColonySampler.Tick` calls\n`DigestVerb.SectionFor(map, \"resources\"|\"power\")` and lifts named scalars out of\nthe dictionaries the digest already builds. Three consequences, all deliberate:\n\n  1. Every accessor is ALREADY AUDITED. This issue's hazard note says a\n     write-on-read bug in a sampler is multiplied rather than incidental; reusing\n     a vetted builder takes that to zero instead of re-auditing it.\n  2. It INHERITS the arithmetic. **`spec/temp-control` is adding a rot term to\n     `food_days` in parallel and nothing in my file needs to know** — I never\n     touched `ResourceSection`. What I assumed: that `resources.food_days`\n     remains a NUMBER under that KEY. If the rot work renames the key or makes\n     it null, my sampler stores NaN for it (holes are dropped from the fit, not\n     fitted through) and `trends.food_days` goes quiet rather than wrong. Suite\n     check 9.13 asserts the key still exists in `ResourceSection`, so a rename\n     fails loudly.\n  3. It is what comment #1's question 2 concluded: the sampler is a persistence\n     layer over an existing observer.\n\nThe 11 columns: food_days, food_nutrition, food_needers, meds, steel, wood,\ncomponents, silver, power_stored_wd, power_gen_w, power_draw_w. Fixed and\nordered; adding a column appends and never renames.\n\nWHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT SAMPLED, each with its reason:\n  - wealth / threat / mood / population — THE GAME ALREADY RECORDS THEM.\n  - hostiles / danger / alerts / downed — those are EVENTS with a tick and a\n    seq, and `Journal.CountsRange` already answers a count over a window.\n  - WEAPONS. Comment #1's strongest result, and I could not close it: no digest\n    section publishes a weapons count, so there is nothing for a sampler to\n    sample. Equipped (pawn scan) and spare (`ThingsInGroup(Weapon)`) are\n    disjoint populations and it is a NEW DERIVATION belonging in the observation\n    surface first. **Filed as git-bug c41bdcc** with an Acceptance section\n    (p2/backlog/wave:4), whose last bullet is \"one row in\n    `ColonySampler.Fields`\". Adding a column late is exactly the case the\n    append-never-rename contract exists to make safe.\n\nCADENCE 2,500 TICKS (one in-game hour), in the order the reasons decide it:\n  1. Divides the vanilla 30,000-tick recorders exactly 12:1, so the two series\n     join with no interpolation.\n  2. Far above the hard floor: `RimWorld/ResourceCounter.ResourceCounterTick` is\n     `if (TicksGame % 204 == 0) UpdateResourceCounts()` and\n     `TotalHumanEdibleNutrition` reads the dictionary it fills, so nothing under\n     `resources.*` moves faster than every 204 ticks.\n  3. 240 samples = a ten-day run, which is this issue's own volume figure.\n  4. Slow enough that the cost is unmeasurable — and it is MEASURED anyway.\n\nRING: 240 entries, flat preallocated arrays (`int[240]` + `double[240*11]`,\n~21 KB), zero allocation per sample after class init. Stride is read from\n`Fields.Length`, not written as a literal.\n\nTICK HOOK: `AgentGameComponent.GameComponentTick`, NEVER `GameComponentUpdate`.\nUpdate runs every FRAME including while PAUSED, so sampling there would append\nidentical rows at a wall-clock rate while the agent sat thinking and flatten\nevery slope with data containing no game time. Suite check 2.3 is the control:\nfour wall-clock seconds paused, zero new points.\n\nWINDOW: 24 points = one in-game day, because a colony's food is periodic on\nexactly that period (meals, sleep, hauling, a hunt returning) and a six-point\nwindow reports the slope of lunch. `trends` takes `window_points` so a caller\ncan ask about a different stretch.\n\nESTIMATOR: ordinary least squares, not an endpoint difference. Colony stocks\nmove in LUMPS, and an endpoint estimate over a window IS one lump at either end\nwhile the regression moves by 1/n. Demonstrated offline in suite check 9.18: on\na series whose true slope is -12/day with one +6 lump at the end, endpoint reads\n-5.74 and the regression -10.56.\n\nTWO FLOORS, and the SPAN one is the guard: \u003e= 3 points AND \u003e= 15,000 ticks of\nspan. Three samples span 5,000 ticks — two in-game hours — and turning that into\na per-day rate is a 12x extrapolation presented as a measurement. Below the\nfloor the answer is `null`, `ready` is false, and `not_ready_why` names the\nfloor that was missed. The first slope of a run therefore arrives at sample 7, a\nquarter of an in-game day in. `span_ticks` rides every slope regardless, so the\nextrapolation factor is never hidden.\n\nCOST: measured by the mod, not promised in a comment — `trends.cost` publishes\n`samples`, `ms_total`, `ms_avg`, `ms_max`, `ticks_covered` and\n`ms_per_1000_ticks`, and names `Journal.cs`'s 0.0039 ms/frame as the benchmark,\nsince the units differ (per frame vs per sample) and a normalised figure is the\nonly comparable one. The suite REPORTS it (2.9) and asserts only a hard \u003c 1 ms\nceiling (2.10), because a suite cannot measure in-tick cost from outside.\n\n=====================================================================\nSAVE / LOAD — SAID LOUDLY, BECAUSE IT GOES THE WAY THAT NEEDS SAYING\n=====================================================================\n\n**THE LIVE RING DOES NOT SURVIVE. THE DURABLE FILE DOES.**\n\nThe ring is cleared at `Runtime.ResetForGameBoundary` — both detectors, beside\n`Placements.Clear()`/`Layouts.Clear()`. Three reasons, first is the real one:\n\n  1. A LOAD CAN MOVE `TicksGame` BACKWARD. A ring carrying samples across that\n     would fit a regression over a discontinuity: points from a future that was\n     rolled back, mixed with points from before it, at overlapping x values.\n     Clearing is the only reading that cannot lie.\n  2. This issue's own acceptance demands the mutation proof be a SAVE-DIFF.\n     Scribing my rows into the save would put this mod's writes inside the diff\n     that exists to prove the mod does not write.\n  3. The durable tier already exists and is better.\n\nThe loss is bounded and VISIBLE rather than silent: after a load `trends.ready`\nis false, `points` is small, `first_tick` is now, and the envelope carries a\n`volatile` sentence saying why. Phases 6/7 measure it and cannot be automated\nend to end (the `b1b3060` precedent — a human saves and loads between them).\n\n=====================================================================\nTHE DURABLE FILE — AND THE DESIGN THAT WAS WRONG FIRST\n=====================================================================\n\n`\u003croot\u003e/samples/\u003csid\u003e.ndjson`. A `header` row declaring the column set, one\n`sample` row per cadence, a `boundary` row at every seam. Written by the POLLER\nthread from a queue the main thread enqueues into, copying `Journal.Flush`'s\npeek-write-dequeue and bounded reopen and NONE of its seq/ring/dedupe/tap\nmachinery.\n\n**IT IS NOT THE JOURNAL, AND THE FIRST DESIGN SAID IT SHOULD BE.** Reusing\n`Journal.Emit` was cheaper and would have broken a guard that shipped this week.\n`722c951`'s unread refusal rests on TimeDriver's own header: \"An advance that\njournaled NOTHING creates no obligation, so a quiet colony never pays for this\nat all.\" A periodic row emitted from inside the tick loop means EVERY advance\nlonger than one cadence journals something, so every subsequent advance refuses\n— converting \"your colony has news you have not read\" into \"time passed\". That\nis the guard's failure mode rather than its purpose, and it would also have been\na change to another spec's contract made silently from a parallel branch. A\nseparate file costs ~90 lines and changes nothing anybody else relies on. Suite\ncheck 9.5 asserts `Journal.Emit` appears nowhere in `ColonySampler.cs`.\n\n=====================================================================\nTHE DIGEST BLOCK, AND ONE HAZARD I AM FLAGGING RATHER THAN HIDING\n=====================================================================\n\n`digest.trends`, ~200 bytes, and a registered predicate section. In the GLANCE\nbecause of the M1 post-mortem's sharpest execution finding — 27 advances, 10\ndigests, ZERO journal calls — so an indicator behind a verb the agent must\nremember to call is one nobody reads. That is session 13's \"a deterministic\nfinding goes in the MOD, not in a note\" one layer up.\n\nCheapest section in the file on session 19's axis: it reads NO game state for\nits own fields (the ring is already in memory) plus `HistorySafe.All()`, which\nis a walk of 3 groups and 11 recorders doing plain field reads. No `Room.Role`,\nno `GetStatValueAbstract`, no pathfind. So this is now affordable and is the\nquestion the harness could not ask:\n\n    advance {until:{condition:{path:\"trends.food_days_per_day\",\n                               op:\"\u003c=\", value:-1.0}}, timeout_ticks:N}\n\nTHE READING IS UP TO ONE CADENCE STALE by construction (`as_of_tick` says how\nstale) — the same kind of floor as the 204-tick ResourceCounter quantum.\n\nTHE HAZARD: `*_to_zero` is null whenever the stock is not falling, and\n`*_per_day` is null until the window fills. `StateWatch.One()` refuses an\nordering operator against null, and the two failure modes are NOT symmetric —\nat ARM time it is a clean refusal naming the reading, but MID-ADVANCE `Poll`\nreturns false and never halts. **So an advance waiting on\n`trends.food_days_to_zero \u003c= 2` stops halting the moment food stops falling —\nexactly when the good news arrives — and runs to its timeout.** Predicates want\n`*_per_day`, which is always a number once `ready` is true. Said in the digest\nsection's own header, in `SectionFor`'s `case \"trends\"`, in DESIGN, in the\nacceptance `.md`, and asserted by suite checks 5.10/5.11, so it cannot quietly\nstop being true.\n\n`food_days_to_zero` is also the number that corrects the game's own overstatement\nthat comment #1 flagged: `food_days` is nutrition per head, the UI calls that\n\"days worth\", and a colonist eats ~1.6 nutrition/day. A MEASURED slope needs no\nsuch assumption — at food_days 3.2 falling 1.6/day the answer is 2.0 days, not\n3.2.\n\n=====================================================================\nACCEPTANCE — accept/2d9a1da-colony-rates.py (+ .md)\n=====================================================================\n\n`--dry-run` plans 90 checks. `--selftest` runs 31 offline, all green.\nPhases 0-5 need a bench; 6/7 are the save/load pair; 9 needs nothing.\n\n  0  shape contract for all three surfaces (every key present, and `shape()`\n     here allows null wherever a slope lives, because null-before-the-window is\n     the CONTRACT and not a missing key)\n  1  the game's series with NO fixture: the wealth identity sample for sample\n     (`WealthWatcher.WealthTotal` IS Items+Buildings+Pawns, so a reader that\n     mis-sliced a tail fails), index-is-tick against the clock, a real\n     30,000-tick boundary crossing growing every 30,000-tick series by exactly\n     one, and agreement with an independent live roster read — caveat stated\n     (the recorder counts caravans and excludes lodgers; `pawns` does neither)\n  2  the sampler on game time; the PAUSED CONTROL (four wall-clock seconds,\n     zero points); the ring bound; the cost; the durable file readable mid-run\n     with its header row\n  3  THE SIGN, both ways, over real elapsed time: 8 rounds up (slope \u003e 0,\n     days_to_zero null AND PRESENT), 8 rounds down (slope \u003c 0, days_to_zero\n     finite positive AND equal to now/-slope from the envelope alone)\n  4  observers never mutate: 30 reads with the CLOCK STOPPED leave the save's\n     `\u003cautoRecorderGroups\u003e` byte-identical (that isolates the READ from time\n     passing); then across a real sampling window the records grow by at most\n     the boundaries the clock crossed — 2.4's method on this recorder\n  5  the predicate surface, including the null trap refusing at arm time\n  6/7 save + human load + the reset, the kept file, the boundary row\n  9  offline: every constant re-derived from source, the estimator against\n     synthetic series, and the REAL banked M1 block\n\nBULLET BY BULLET:\n\n  - sample row on a defined cadence, field set fixed and documented, file\n    readable mid-run ....................................... COVERED (2.11-2.18)\n  - overhead measured against Journal's 0.0039 ms/frame and reported\n    ........................ COVERED (2.9/2.10) — the MOD publishes the number\n  - every field marked game-recorded / digest-already / new derivation\n    ..... COVERED — and it is in the DATA, not only the docs: every `trends`\n    row carries `from: \"digest.\u003csection\u003e.\u003ckey\u003e\"` and `history` carries `source`\n  - a series from which food_days' SLOPE is computable ........ COVERED (3.3,\n    3.9, 3.10, 3.11), both signs, over real elapsed game time\n  - ...shown BEFORE the vanilla alert fires ......... PARTIALLY. The slope half\n    is covered. The \"beats the alert\" comparison is a NOTE (3.15), not an\n    assertion, and I am saying so rather than dressing it up: `Alert_LowFood\n    .GetReport` opens `if (TicksGame \u003c 150000f) return false;`, so on a young\n    colony the alert has no lagging side to beat, and on an old one staging a\n    real starvation is a ten-day fixture, not a suite phase. **ORCHESTRATOR-\n    MANUAL if a live starvation run is wanted as evidence** — the numbers to\n    bank are `trends.food_days_per_day` and `food_days_to_zero` at the tick\n    Alert_LowFood first appears in `digest.alerts.active`.\n  - zero red errors ..................... ORCHESTRATOR-MANUAL (bench invariant)\n  - no accessor mutates on read, save-diff around a sampling window\n    ................................................ COVERED (4.3, 4.4, 4.8)\n\nFIXTURE RECIPE: a colony, paused, devMode on, and **a stockpile zone that\naccepts meals** — `digest.resources.*` is stockpile-only, so food on unzoned\nground reads as zero nutrition and a series staged there never moves. Phase 3\npreconditions on `dev:spawn-thing {stockpile:true}` reporting\n`pos_source == \"stockpile\"` and exits 2 with the recipe if it does not.\n`--food-def` overrides `MealSimple`. Phase 4 leaves rates-diff-a/-b/-c.rws on\nthe bench (evidence until graded). Budget a few minutes of Ultrafast: phase 1\nadvances up to ~30,000 ticks, phase 3 ~42,000 across 16 rounds, phase 4 ~15,400.\n\nWAITS: every one is a clock predicate with the target read off the game, every\n`until` carries `timeout_ticks` (1113019). The header says why that is right\nhere and was not in 722c951: this suite waits for a defined amount of GAME time\nand the game is PAUSED between calls, so there is no race to lose.\n\nTWO THINGS THE SUITE FOUND IN ITSELF, kept as worked examples rather than\nquietly fixed: `shape()` had to allow null wherever a slope lives; and\n`code_only()` had to strip STRING LITERALS as well as comments, because check\n9.8 (\"never reads LabelCap\") failed on the verb's own provenance string — the\ncheck was failing the file for documenting what it does not call.\n\nNOTHING HERE HAS MET A BENCH.\n\nONE UNRELATED OBSERVATION, for whoever owns the digest: several shipped suites\ncall `send(\"digest\", {\"sections\": [\"time\"]})`, and `digest` reads only `since`\nand `colonists_cap`. The call works (the full digest comes back) but `sections`\nis a stray key reported in `ignored_args` on every one of those calls. Either\n`digest` should take `sections` — the machinery exists, `SectionFor` is right\nthere — or the suites should stop passing it. Not filed as an issue because it\nis a one-line question for the digest's owner rather than a defect with a\nfixture; say the word and I will file it.","files":null}]}